{:edb/db-before-t 382 :edb/db-after-t 383 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533695 50 #inst "2026-10-04T17:25:38.900Z" 13194139533695 true] [17592186073751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247406 13194139533695 true] [17592186073751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247455 13194139533695 true] [17592186073751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247462 13194139533695 true] [17592186073751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247469 13194139533695 true] [17592186073752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247420 13194139533695 true] [17592186073752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247427 13194139533695 true] [17592186073752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247441 13194139533695 true] [17592186073752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247448 13194139533695 true] [17592186073752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247476 13194139533695 true] [17592186073756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247413 13194139533695 true] [17592186073756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247434 13194139533695 true] [17592186073756 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247483 13194139533695 true] [17592186074454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533695 true] [17592186074454 1145 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/f907ee8b0bb92792cc69905678210af71088af6731e5a586eaeed0bfc7856b9c.png)\n\nSince the line $y=2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(5,-1)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-2|=3.\n$$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186074457 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533695 true] [17592186074457 1145 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186074458 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533695 true] [17592186074458 1145 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247407 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1089 "q-114323" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(4,3)$ and the line $x=2.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1113 #uuid "f162095a-e7b4-52b0-b16f-7a98cc60e6ca" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247406 1145 "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/ea64c0752738b0c917d897060351cdd43ed7bbf687cc20781b7241ee3be0ad3c.png)\n\nSince the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(4,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|4-2|=2.\n$$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247408 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247409 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247410 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247411 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247412 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247410 13194139533695 true] [17592186247407 1110 #uuid "ce57ccc5-4582-5aa3-b24d-c0f2fe9b4412" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247408 1034 "3" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247408 1111 #uuid "41a60c3d-1754-566b-8910-cffc9ce6f802" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247409 1034 "7" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247409 1111 #uuid "204d018e-c16b-530e-bf7a-b75c329fc52d" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247410 1034 "2" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247410 1111 #uuid "d784237f-8ede-5b02-88fc-86614228987d" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247411 1034 "1" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247411 1111 #uuid "86ba8fd1-d20d-5cf0-b2d4-7a170b627a5c" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247412 1034 "4" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247412 1111 #uuid "8fce9667-6b42-5bc2-8e5a-dddedd0cb878" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247414 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1089 "q-114336" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1113 #uuid "49e0ab8c-4ebb-5b2d-8e5e-32400720dc1f" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247413 1145 "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247415 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247416 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247417 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247418 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247419 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247418 13194139533695 true] [17592186247414 1110 #uuid "cfe32756-a5c1-5bb5-a38a-24fb672f9c74" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247415 1034 "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247415 1111 #uuid "51f5f015-9087-5c4e-b535-bd49c4b2f589" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247416 1034 "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247416 1111 #uuid "f0ade176-cbf4-587c-a5d8-17992475a05c" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247417 1034 "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247417 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247417 1111 #uuid "c78e19f3-70d9-56c1-a6ea-50b787d3887a" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247418 1034 "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247418 1111 #uuid "479c1d82-775d-5218-a0f2-3f2d9dfb1035" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247419 1034 "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247419 1111 #uuid "e8902a36-ed80-5987-9b7a-267319183572" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247421 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1089 "q-114341" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1113 #uuid "e79577df-8880-5651-be09-d6a38cdd29b6" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247420 1145 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/3a4bbfd80480fe3fcc778a490514d27d65d83abf77ca1752e4203d9b3185b618.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(1,-5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{17}{3}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(4,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,-5)$ and $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-1)^{2}+(-3+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247422 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247423 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247424 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247425 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247426 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247423 13194139533695 true] [17592186247421 1110 #uuid "47c2e812-cb6a-5580-bf28-e45b01817124" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247422 1034 "4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247422 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247422 1111 #uuid "2b83b79a-3d27-563d-817a-17fce0796043" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247423 1034 "\\sqrt{13}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247423 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247423 1111 #uuid "a3ee4382-b711-529c-bafa-5337cfc35ccb" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247424 1034 "25" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247424 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247424 1111 #uuid "9ff7d472-b785-5d11-9101-4d8e4f862c6b" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247425 1034 "5" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247425 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247425 1111 #uuid "b3301e55-6155-51ca-a280-86552f2bdaa3" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247426 1034 "3\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247426 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247426 1111 #uuid "431e99be-c9e2-5789-b4b7-9db2caaab681" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247428 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1089 "q-114343" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1113 #uuid "c8a6f517-e603-56a5-8d4e-8cbb84ce1587" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247427 1145 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247429 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247430 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247431 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247432 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247433 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247429 13194139533695 true] [17592186247428 1110 #uuid "e728ae88-bf1b-58c8-968b-04a4a468a486" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247429 1034 "4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247429 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247429 1111 #uuid "36bebfca-66e3-536e-b2d7-230ae61652b1" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247430 1034 "2" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247430 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247430 1111 #uuid "16fd2dc9-2117-5499-971e-3acc531cba0d" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247431 1034 "2\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247431 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194