}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247436 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247437 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247438 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247439 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247440 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247439 13194139533695 true] [17592186247435 1110 #uuid "c6958058-789f-5cc4-aa89-27ad2f2cdceb" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247436 1034 "(3,11)" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247436 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247436 1111 #uuid "f91581f5-1f72-5dae-88c1-f6bcfb29be2b" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247437 1034 "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247437 1111 #uuid "416bff52-2ca3-5809-9dd3-06101b21863e" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247438 1034 "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247438 1111 #uuid "400691fb-900d-5318-9bfd-096e5742224a" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247439 1034 "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247439 1111 #uuid "58f5b96f-8e0f-5d43-be42-2be5f5478d64" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247440 1034 "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247440 1111 #uuid "d518c73f-060d-5a2e-a88b-ccd0767ccf2e" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247442 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1089 "q-114358" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1113 #uuid "b9d5b1c1-cc35-5675-83c0-9258962522cd" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247441 1145 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247443 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247444 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247445 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247446 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247447 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247443 13194139533695 true] [17592186247442 1110 #uuid "86a13e16-d3cc-55f3-9310-c97d9b334a7c" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247443 1034 "\\sqrt{29}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247443 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247443 1111 #uuid "22435a36-a01f-5169-853b-df19b9d7fd4f" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247444 1034 "2\\sqrt{7}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247444 1111 #uuid "9cd5714e-c909-5212-813a-93836ab3a06f" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247445 1034 "\\sqrt{19}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247445 1111 #uuid "9b3a4a11-d48d-5cfe-aa22-5c2868265ad4