system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247451 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247452 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247454 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247449 1110 #uuid "8e3ceee1-bacb-5d42-91fb-82c9f4a68f0e" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247450 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247450 1111 #uuid "a0ba03f5-5011-5c62-abe1-b762cb17cff2" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247451 1034 "\\sqrt{10}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247451 1111 #uuid "1d9f84bd-11d4-5ee3-8592-2e261036f7d5" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247452 1034 "3" 13194139533695 true] [1759