4 "5" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247475 1111 #uuid "e93e5e44-de73-5bd5-89b9-078bfc554789" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247477 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1089 "q-74417" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1100 "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1113 #uuid "1a823c10-5ebd-503e-9bab-03ff8b0369b1" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247476 1145 "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247478 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247479 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247480 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247481 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247482 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1041 "selection" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247482 13194139533695 true] [17592186247477 1110 #uuid "a95cdb93-c8e3-5a95-a41d-02d781042020" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247478 1034 "9" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247478 1111 #uuid "a6255463-5820-582f-b710-6cb9ce7f0d9b" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247479 1034 "3" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247479 1111 #uuid "fc889719-669e-5057-a251-e51ea4da88f1" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247480 1034 "3\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247480 1111 #uuid "f21c6d77-c735-5067-b312-7a5d2c649ff8" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247481 1034 "9\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247481 1111 #uuid "7fc122d5-1d1e-5697-a372-a904ded26a48" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247482 1034 "3\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533695 true] [17592186247482 1111 #uuid "18bd2741-5630-52c0-b65b-c084ea181bc1" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247484 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1089 "q-74439" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1113 #uuid "942dc2e8-6f1d-5b4e-8dc9-4a3941397629" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1138 false 13194139533695 true] [17592186247483 1145 "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$" 13194139533695 true] [17592186247484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247485 13194139533695 true] [17592186247484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247486 13194139533695 true] [17592186247484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247487 13194139533695 true] [17592186247484 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247488 13194139533695 true] [17592186247484 1000 #